        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.111,
            0.884,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "**The Midpoint Theorem:** the line joining the two midpoints of any two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and has half the length of the third side."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.174,
            0.881,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "**A Converse to the Midpoint Theorem:** the line passing through the midpoint of a side of a triangle and parallel to a second side bisects the third side."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.237,
            0.88,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "Medians of a triangle are concurrent and the point of concurrence, called the **centroid**, divides each median in a 2 : 1 ratio."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.281,
            0.881,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "The 4-gon formed by joining the midpoints of a given 4-gon is always a parallelogram."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.325,
            0.663,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "• The plane can be tiled with any given 4-gon."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
